WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.950
British pubs have survived
wars, floods, fires,

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.630
and more than one plague.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.580
The question now
is can they weather

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.470
the coronavirus pandemic?

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.079
Everyone is wondering
whether the pub industry

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.650
is on the verge of collapse.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.570
So what this chart shows
us is spending in UK pubs

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.020
compared to the same
day of the week in 2019.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.860
Zero here is the baseline.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.040
You can see that it
dropped by 100 per cent

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.040
- so completely -
during the lockdown.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.170
And then, as it reopened,
spending actually

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:42.090
exceeded what happened in 2019
in the months between August

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.120
and even just before the
beginning of October.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.800
Pubs had to be closed.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.120
There really wasn't a choice.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.850
Those people who tested
positive for coronavirus

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.120
were twice as likely to
have been at a restaurant

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.890
than those who tested negative.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.670
And in the chart we can see it
in the blue bar, dark blue bar.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.820
And that was also
true in the UK.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.240
We've got here some data from
Public Health England that

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.410
shows that shopping
and eating out

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.610
were the activities most
likely to be reported

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.500
amongst those people who tested
positive for coronavirus.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.160
So the same rings
true for the UK.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.320
When the case count
started to drop,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.490
the chancellor,
Rishi Sunak, decided

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.850
that once pubs had
reopened, he would actually

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.430
actively incentivise
people to go to pubs.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.800
So people were given a
discount and encouraged

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.550
to eat in pubs and
restaurants, and bars and pubs

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.740
were the venues
that had the most

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.990
windfall from this activity.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.070
But these venues are hotspots
for coronavirus transmissions,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.010
so that's why we're here today
on the last day of pubs being

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.440
open in England before a
nationwide lockdown that

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.760
should last a month.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.590
I don't have a crystal ball
to tell you what happens next.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.760
But it's reasonable to expect
that amongst the millions

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.760
of people who work in the
hospitality industry and who

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.790
are more likely to
be young - under 35 -

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.670
women and in a part-time
and low-paid jobs,

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.950
a lot of them might
not come back to a job.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.830
We know that the people
who have been furloughed

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.700
are more likely to be low paid,
as we can see from this chart.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.620
Also, this chart
really shows us what's

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.110
happened to the
hospitality industry.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.550
This is quite indicative.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.200
It's weekly hours of work.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.870
And we can see that
the pink line, which

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.270
is workers in the
hospitality sector,

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.042
have been more affected
than the average.

00:02:27.042 --> 00:02:28.500
I think it's
important to point out

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.120
a bit of historical context.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.280
The number of public
houses in the UK

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.020
have been declining for a while.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.490
So we've gotten this chart here
- number of pubs since 2001.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.195
There's actually
two main events that

00:02:40.195 --> 00:02:42.840
affected the decline
in the number of pubs

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.250
and accelerated it.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.520
One is the ban on
smoking indoors in pubs,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.960
and the other one is
the financial crisis.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.640
So you can see that we went
from well over 50,000 pubs

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.450
in the early 2000s
to about 39,000 now.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.640
According to estimates,
the number of pubs

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.407
could decline by 25
per cent in the future.

00:03:02.407 --> 00:03:04.740
However, we do know... from
this chart that I showed you

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.990
earlier... that British
people are actually very loyal

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.380
to pubs, and they're happy to
flock back when they reopen

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.960
and spend even more than
they did in previous years.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.260
So if you're feeling very
gloomy about the future of pubs

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.040
perhaps it's not all bad news.

